==Enemy==
Essentially a Traitless/Behemoth [[Relic Doge]], Wild Doge is faster than it and has even higher DPS, Area Attack, a 30% chance to survive a lethal strike, immunity to [[Surge Attack]]s and almost no backswing, making him one of the strongest [[Doge]] variants when it comes to pushing. In odd contrast to the bulk of Doge's other variants, he also has 150 range, meaning he has more range than most meatshields (usually from 110 to 140) rather than being outranged himself (this includes units such as [[Slime Cat (Rare Cat)|Jellycat]] and [[Teacher BearCat (Rare Cat)|Teacher BearCat]], so timing may be required).

As Wild Doge is considered Traitless, any [[Special Abilities|abilities]] targeting "all traits" or "Traited enemies" (e.g., [[Shining Amaterasu (Uber Rare Cat)|Shining Amaterasu]]'s Massive Damage, [[Baby Gao (Uber Rare Cat)|Baby Gao]]'s Strong Against, [[Wafer Cat (Special Cat)|Wafer Cat]]'s Slow) will not activate against Wild Doge. On the otherhand, abilities specifically targeting Traitless or Behemoth enemies ([[Shadow Gao (Uber Rare Cat)|Shadow Gao]]'s Massive Damage, [[Ancient Egg: N101 (Rare Cat)|Courier Cat]]'s Behemoth Slayer) continue to function per usual. Units with both Behemoth Slayer and stat-boosting anti-Traitless abilities can stack, as in the case of [[Ancient Egg: N001 (Special Cat)|Haniwa Cat]] and [[Ancient Egg: N111 (Super Rare Cat)|Cop Cat]].

==Strategy==
Being immune to Surges changes little unless you rely heavily on [[:Category:Surge Attack Cats|Surge Attackers]] to win stages; the real problem comes from his extreme pushing power and ability to survive an attack from [[Bahamut Cat (Special Cat)|Awakened Bahamut Cat]] or other hard-hitting units and then land a hit on them. Otherwise, treat Wild Doge like you would any of Doge's stronger variants, but with longer range and no trait. Without [[:Category:Anti-Traitless Cats|anti-Traitless Cats]], the best strategy is to wear him down with [[:Category:Area Attack Cats|Area Attackers]] like [[Mer-Cat (Rare Cat)|Cameraman Cat]], [[Salon Cat (Rare Cat)|Paris Cat]] and [[Crazed Bird Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Crazed UFO Cat]]. [[Ururun Wolf (Special Cat)|Ururun]] and [[Urs & Fenrir (Special Cat)|Urs]] could be especially good for controlling hordes of them. [[Ancient Egg: N101 (Rare Cat)|Courier Cat]] can deal good damage reliably, but its Long Distance ability necessitates having some support. In [[True Form]], [[Dogumaru (Special Cat)|Reborn Warrior Doguemon]] can not only resist Wild Doge, but, with good survive luck, one-shot him at 200% at level 33, giving you an excellent counter to them in late-game.

If you have him, a level 50 [[Ancient Egg: N112 (Rare Cat)|Cat Egg Pod]] can one-tap Wild Doge at 200% and has high dodge chance and hp to live easily if Wild Doge's survive triggers.

Buffed Wild Doges in general can be a major threat without high-level cats. While [[Ancient Egg: N006 (Special Cat)|Fallen Bear Cat]]'s Behemoth Slayer ability is usually inconsequential, it outranges Wild Doge and gains enough damage to instantly knockback a 200% Wild Doge at level 49, which even Courier Cat cannot do without [[Cat Combo|Combos]]. Defensively, even though Wild Doge isn't a [[:Category:Dark Enemies|Dark]] enemy, [[Li'l Gau (Rare Cat)|Crystal Li'l Gau Gau]] is a surprisingly viable meatshield at level 50+13, tanking one hit from a 200% Wild Doge while having almost the shortest recharge time possible.

